WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.211 --> 00:00:04.694
<v Maya>Let's talk about a fear that is probably creeping into the back of your mind lately.</v>

2
00:00:05.074 --> 00:00:06.735
<v Daniel>Oh, yeah. The whole hermit scenario.</v>

3
00:00:06.955 --> 00:00:21.745
<v Maya>Right. Exactly. There is this really widespread assumption out there that artificial intelligence is just going to turn knowledge workers into these isolated button pushing hermits. You know the scenario, right? It's just you and a glowing screen generating reports all day without ever</v>

4
00:00:21.845 --> 00:00:23.526
<v Maya>actually speaking to another human being.</v>

5
00:00:23.786 --> 00:00:28.209
<v Daniel>Yeah, it's a very common fear. We naturally view automation as this tool for isolation.</v>

6
00:00:28.866 --> 00:00:36.353
<v Maya>But what if the data shows the exact opposite? Like, what if generative AI actually forces us to talk to each other way more?</v>

7
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:46.342
<v Daniel>It completely flips the conventional wisdom on its head, honestly. The reality of how we integrate these cognitive tools into a living, breathing office is proving to be, well, a lot messier.</v>

8
00:00:46.953 --> 00:01:01.897
<v Maya>Right, because if people are relying on AI to do the talking for them, you'd think the human network would shrink. But a team from Bocconi University just published a paper that completely shatters that assumption. So today we are taking a deep dive into a brand new paper titled The Effects of</v>

9
00:01:02.017 --> 00:01:05.938
<v Maya>Generative AI on Collaboration and Social Networks Among Knowledge Workers.</v>

10
00:01:06.578 --> 00:01:13.340
<v Daniel>And that's by Giuseppe Soda, Alessandro Iorio, and Leonardo Rizzo. It was just posted to archive on October 23rd, 2026.</v>

11
00:01:14.709 --> 00:01:23.214
<v Maya>Yes, thank you. And our mission today is to extract the absolute most important insights from this so you can be the most well-informed person in the room.</v>

12
00:01:23.955 --> 00:01:33.680
<v Daniel>Though, before we really get into their findings, it is crucial to state clearly, exactly once, that this is a working paper and has not yet been peer-reviewed.</v>

13
00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:34.481
<v Maya>Right, good point.</v>

14
00:01:34.961 --> 00:01:41.325
<v Daniel>It gives us an incredibly detailed early look at a phenomenon as it unfolds, but we just have to keep that context in mind.</v>

15
00:01:41.672 --> 00:01:53.880
<v Maya>Duly noted. So the researchers set out to test two competing predictions about what happens when you drop generative AI into a modern office. Prediction one is what they call relational displacement.</v>

16
00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:55.761
<v Daniel>That's the hermit scenario you mentioned.</v>

17
00:01:55.961 --> 00:02:06.508
<v Maya>Exactly. A worker asks the AI assistant a question instead of asking a human colleague, shrinking their overall collaboration network. Prediction two, on the other hand, is expansion.</v>

18
00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:09.432
<v Daniel>Right, which is super counterintuitive at first.</v>

19
00:02:09.572 --> 00:02:24.462
<v Maya>It is. It's this idea that AI outputs sound incredibly fluent, but they are fundamentally opaque because you don't really know how the AI got its answer. You actually need to expand your collaboration to get your human colleagues to interpret, check,</v>

20
00:02:24.982 --> 00:02:27.444
<v Maya>and, you know, legitimize the AI's work.</v>

21
00:02:28.124 --> 00:02:33.368
<v Daniel>It really comes down to the tension between the ease of generating an answer and the difficulty of actually trusting it.</v>

22
00:02:33.951 --> 00:02:42.056
<v Maya>Think of it like using a calculator versus hiring a ghostwriter. You don't need to ask your buddy to check your calculator's math. The calculator is transparent.</v>

23
00:02:42.276 --> 00:02:43.657
<v Daniel>Yeah, nobody double-checks a calculator.</v>

24
00:02:43.917 --> 00:02:58.867
<v Maya>Right. But you absolutely need them to read the ghostwriter's weird first draft to make sure it actually sounds like you and makes sense. So to figure out which one generative AI resembles, we really have to look at what we thought we knew about AI and remote work before this paper</v>

25
00:02:58.907 --> 00:02:59.187
<v Maya>dropped.</v>

26
00:02:59.843 --> 00:03:09.887
<v Daniel>Yeah, the historical contrast is really important here. Back in 2024, there was this major meta-analysis by Vaccaro, Almatuk, and Malone at MIT.</v>

27
00:03:10.007 --> 00:03:11.068
<v Maya>Right, the dyad study.</v>

28
00:03:11.288 --> 00:03:20.252
<v Daniel>Exactly. They looked at over 100 experimental studies and found that human plus AI combinations often did worse than the best of the two working alone.</v>

29
00:03:20.872 --> 00:03:27.535
<v Maya>Which, I mean, that sounds terrible for AI adoption on its face. But the flaw there is that nobody works alone in a room.</v>

30
00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:37.616
<v Daniel>Right. That is the critical blind spot in that older research. Those studies almost exclusively examined the person and the machine as an isolated pair, a dyad.</v>

31
00:03:37.996 --> 00:03:40.237
<v Maya>Just a human and a bot in a vacuum.</v>

32
00:03:40.437 --> 00:03:51.824
<v Daniel>Yes. They put them in a vacuum and measure the output. But what this new Bocconi paper does is shift the unit of analysis entirely. They are looking at the entire surrounding network of colleagues.</v>

33
00:03:52.679 --> 00:04:01.131
<v Maya>Because we all have bosses and peers and compliance departments. But to be fair, the fear of technology isolating us didn't just come from thin air, right?</v>

34
00:04:01.531 --> 00:04:09.282
<v Daniel>Oh, not at all. The authors and I think the broader public had a very recent historical parallel in mind. The shift to remote work.</v>

35
00:04:10.111 --> 00:04:11.311
<v Maya>Yeah, that Microsoft study.</v>

36
00:04:11.591 --> 00:04:24.615
<v Daniel>Right. The massive 2022 Microsoft study by Yang, Holt, Jaffe, and colleagues. They analyzed over 60,000 workers and showed that firm-wide remote work made collaboration networks significantly more static and siloed.</v>

37
00:04:25.335 --> 00:04:28.016
<v Maya>Right. There were fewer bridges between different groups.</v>

38
00:04:28.497 --> 00:04:36.182
<v Daniel>Exactly. And communication heavily shifted from synchronous methods like calls and meetings to asynchronous methods like emails.</v>

39
00:04:36.422 --> 00:04:50.932
<v Maya>OK, let's unpack this for a second. So going into this new study, the baseline expectation is that AI acts just like remote work. The ultimate asynchronous assistant that never sleeps should basically push people further into their silos, right? Just totally siloed workers sending fewer</v>

40
00:04:51.412 --> 00:04:54.154
<v Maya>meeting invites and retreating into their own digital corners.</v>

41
00:04:54.434 --> 00:04:55.855
<v Daniel>That was exactly the expectation.</v>

42
00:04:56.390 --> 00:05:02.712
<v Maya>But to actually prove or disprove that displacement pattern, the researchers needed a massive real-world laboratory.</v>

43
00:05:03.052 --> 00:05:04.252
<v Daniel>And they totally found one.</v>

44
00:05:04.672 --> 00:05:12.534
<v Maya>The data set here is wild. They tracked 12,040 employees in one single business unit of a large multinational consumer products company.</v>

45
00:05:12.794 --> 00:05:13.775
<v Daniel>A huge sample size.</v>

46
00:05:13.855 --> 00:05:21.957
<v Maya>Yeah, and they observed them every single month from September 2024 to September 2025. This was right during a staged rollout of Microsoft Copilot.</v>

47
00:05:22.535 --> 00:05:30.439
<v Daniel>Now, the data caveats here are crucial for understanding the study's power and its ethical boundaries, frankly. The data comes from Microsoft Viva Insights.</v>

48
00:05:30.679 --> 00:05:32.600
<v Maya>Which means it only tracks metadata.</v>

49
00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:41.984
<v Daniel>Yes, exactly. It logs the existence of meetings, calls, emails, and chats and their duration, but it never captures the actual content of the communication.</v>

50
00:05:42.440 --> 00:05:50.282
<v Maya>So they know I spent an hour in a meeting with you, but they have zero idea if we were discussing the Q3 strategy or, you know, ranking our favorite pizza toppings.</v>

51
00:05:50.622 --> 00:05:58.564
<v Daniel>Exactly. And for the purposes of mapping the network, they defined a tie between two people as at least one hour of collaboration in a given month.</v>

52
00:05:58.664 --> 00:05:59.544
<v Maya>Okay, that makes sense.</v>

53
00:06:00.304 --> 00:06:12.327
<v Daniel>And importantly, Copilot usage and collaboration metrics are logged by two entirely separate systems within Microsoft. So using Copilot doesn't accidentally trigger a collaboration log just because it's the same software suite.</v>

54
00:06:12.767 --> 00:06:27.613
<v Maya>Okay. So there's no data overlap artifact. That's a really great technical sanity check. But how are they actually defining an adopter? Because, I mean, I know plenty of people who tried Copilot once, asked it to write a C shanty about</v>

55
00:06:27.673 --> 00:06:29.553
<v Maya>spreadsheets, and then just never opened it again.</v>

56
00:06:29.793 --> 00:06:30.934
<v Daniel>Right. The novelty users.</v>

57
00:06:31.014 --> 00:06:34.115
<v Maya>Yeah. That doesn't make them a real AI integrated worker.</v>

58
00:06:34.533 --> 00:06:45.798
<v Daniel>And to filter out that exact scenario, the researchers set a very rigorous threshold. A worker only counted as an adopter after two consecutive months of copilot use above the 25th percentile.</v>

59
00:06:45.898 --> 00:06:46.218
<v Maya>Got it.</v>

60
00:06:47.058 --> 00:06:58.463
<v Daniel>They wanted to measure sustained, meaningful integration into the workflow. So out of the roughly 12,000 employees, the final sample gave them 1,992 sustained adopters and 9,876 never adopters to act as a control group.</v>

61
00:07:04.523 --> 00:07:06.025
<v Maya>And didn't they drop a few people too?</v>

62
00:07:06.405 --> 00:07:14.593
<v Daniel>They did. They dropped 172 workers who were already using Copilot at the very start of the observation period, just to keep the baseline clean.</v>

63
00:07:14.833 --> 00:07:26.925
<v Maya>Okay, so you have your two groups, but here is the tricky part. How do you prove that the AI actually caused a change in behavior? I mean, maybe chatty, highly collaborative people are simply the ones who also love trying new software.</v>

64
00:07:27.548 --> 00:07:40.976
<v Daniel>That is the classic correlation versus causation problem. And to solve it, the researchers used two statistical designs. The first is a staggered difference in differences model, specifically using the Callaway and Santana method.</v>

65
00:07:41.857 --> 00:07:43.558
<v Maya>Okay, what does that mean in plain English?</v>

66
00:07:44.218 --> 00:07:55.505
<v Daniel>Well, because Copilot rolled out in stages, they could compare adopters before and after they got the tool against the never adopters during that exact same time frame. And they found what we call flat pre-trends.</v>

67
00:07:56.569 --> 00:08:05.152
<v Maya>Oh, meaning that before the AI was introduced, both groups were acting on the exact same trajectory. The future adopters weren't already secretly ramping up their meeting hours.</v>

68
00:08:05.833 --> 00:08:16.557
<v Daniel>Exactly. The behavioral divergence only happened after the tool was actually introduced. The second design was even more interesting. It's a shift-share instrument, or an IV model.</v>

69
00:08:17.577 --> 00:08:17.877
<v Maya>Okay.</v>

70
00:08:18.454 --> 00:08:32.844
<v Daniel>Think of it as looking for a random shock to the system. They looked at the monthly intensity of Microsoft's own co-pilot feature releases, that's the shift, and multiplied that by how many of each worker's collaborators were already co-pilot users at the start,</v>

71
00:08:33.384 --> 00:08:34.365
<v Daniel>that's the share.</v>

72
00:08:35.035 --> 00:08:45.467
<v Maya>Wow. Okay. So we are basically tracking the digital footprint of the conversation, not the words themselves, and using Microsoft's own software updates as a randomized stress test.</v>

73
00:08:45.767 --> 00:08:45.967
<v Daniel>Yes.</v>

74
00:08:46.468 --> 00:08:55.618
<v Maya>If Microsoft pushes a massive new feature, the people who are heavily surrounded by co-pilot users are going to feel the blast radius of that update way more than isolated users.</v>

75
00:08:55.852 --> 00:09:10.839
<v Daniel>That's a perfect way to put it. And statistically, this stress test proved to be incredibly robust. They note that the first stage is strong, meaning the instrument reliably predicted actual usage. They gave it an F statistic of about 80, which in statistics is essentially an</v>

76
00:09:10.999 --> 00:09:12.440
<v Daniel>A plus grade for reliability.</v>

77
00:09:12.600 --> 00:09:12.860
<v Maya>Wow.</v>

78
00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:23.445
<v Daniel>Furthermore, when they ran placebo tests on the never adopters using this same shock, they found absolutely nothing. The blast radius only affected collaboration through actual copilot use.</v>

79
00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:37.349
<v Maya>That is fascinating. And if you are someone who manages a team, this methodology is incredibly important because it proves that simply buying software licenses and flipping a switch actively changes the social fabric of your office.</v>

80
00:09:37.509 --> 00:09:38.130
<v Daniel>It really does.</v>

81
00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:48.757
<v Maya>Which leads to the core question. So with all these people using Copilot, I'd expect their meeting calendars to just completely clear out. What does this all mean? Did the network shrink or expand?</v>

82
00:09:49.275 --> 00:09:53.216
<v Daniel>Their calendars did not clear out. Collaboration expanded.</v>

83
00:09:53.476 --> 00:09:54.016
<v Maya>Wait, really?</v>

84
00:09:54.276 --> 00:10:05.958
<v Daniel>Yes. The headline statistic is that self-initiated or what the researchers call generated collaboration rises by about 1.4 hours a month on average over the first three months after adoption.</v>

85
00:10:06.198 --> 00:10:21.157
<v Maya>Okay, I do want to jump in here for a quick honesty check on those stats because I saw this in the paper. That 1.4 hours is specifically for the first three months. Right. If you look at the overall average effect across all cohorts for the entire time period, it was about</v>

86
00:10:21.417 --> 00:10:28.280
<v Maya>0.95 hours. And that number is technically not statistically significant. So why focus on the three-month window?</v>

87
00:10:28.540 --> 00:10:39.744
<v Daniel>Yeah, it's an important distinction. The authors prefer that three-month average because they argue that is the window when the behavioral shift actually occurs. It captures the shock of integrating the tool into daily routines.</v>

88
00:10:40.364 --> 00:10:40.684
<v Maya>Ah, okay.</v>

89
00:10:41.304 --> 00:10:47.249
<v Daniel>And when you look at total collaboration, which includes incoming requests from others, the rise is robust across the board.</v>

90
00:10:47.989 --> 00:10:52.253
<v Maya>Did it push everyone into asynchronous emails, though, like the way remote work did?</v>

91
00:10:52.573 --> 00:11:06.564
<v Daniel>Actually, no. Both synchronous time, meaning meetings and calls, and asynchronous time, like emails, go up. However, the larger short-run response is actually in those synchronous meetings and calls. People are picking up the phone and scheduling time to talk live.</v>

92
00:11:06.951 --> 00:11:10.895
<v Maya>Which creates a really fascinating tension with some of the background research on this.</v>

93
00:11:11.075 --> 00:11:11.616
<v Daniel>Oh, absolutely.</v>

94
00:11:11.936 --> 00:11:26.751
<v Maya>Let's bring in that 2025 study by Dillon and colleagues, the early impacts of M365 copilot paper. Dillon ran a randomized experiment with over 6,000 workers in 56 different firms. They found copilot users spent about half an</v>

95
00:11:26.791 --> 00:11:41.698
<v Maya>hour less a week reading emails, and they finished documents faster. Right. But their meeting times didn't fall. So is this new Bocconi paper's finding of rising collaboration consistent with that? Like, are people saving time reading long emails just to schedule more</v>

96
00:11:41.718 --> 00:11:42.158
<v Maya>meetings?</v>

97
00:11:42.598 --> 00:11:55.821
<v Daniel>The two findings align perfectly, actually. Dylan's study showed that AI makes you faster at processing information. You know, summarizing a long email thread saves you time. But the Bocconi paper shows us where that save time goes.</v>

98
00:11:56.587 --> 00:11:57.508
<v Maya>You reinvest it.</v>

99
00:11:57.808 --> 00:12:09.414
<v Daniel>Yes. You reinvest it into interpersonal coordination. You aren't spending your day reading endless update emails. Instead, you're hopping on a quick call to debate the AI-generated strategy document you just produced.</v>

100
00:12:10.441 --> 00:12:14.785
<v Maya>And their social circles are widening, right? They aren't just calling the same three co-workers on repeat?</v>

101
00:12:15.125 --> 00:12:28.458
<v Daniel>Exactly. Adopters start working with about two to three more distinct colleagues every single month. Their networks become less clustered, meaning they are reaching beyond their tight, usual local work groups and building bridges across the organization.</v>

102
00:12:28.761 --> 00:12:32.603
<v Maya>That becomes even more pronounced when you look at that stress test model you mentioned earlier.</v>

103
00:12:32.623 --> 00:12:33.823
<v Daniel>The IV model, yeah.</v>

104
00:12:34.023 --> 00:12:44.608
<v Maya>Right. In the IV models, a tenfold increase in copilot actions goes with about 3.4 more collaboration hours and about 20 more distinct colleagues a month.</v>

105
00:12:44.628 --> 00:12:46.049
<v Daniel>It's a massive multiplier.</v>

106
00:12:46.669 --> 00:12:54.633
<v Maya>And I noticed in the data that if they dropped never adopters who were directly connected to the copilot users, the effects actually got larger, not smaller.</v>

107
00:12:54.957 --> 00:13:01.341
<v Daniel>Right, which confirms that the effect is driven directly by the AI usage, not just by spillover noise in the immediate network.</v>

108
00:13:02.061 --> 00:13:11.807
<v Maya>So we know collaboration is expanding. The bottleneck of work has clearly moved, but why? If AI makes us exponentially faster at producing work, why are we talking more?</v>

109
00:13:12.348 --> 00:13:16.050
<v Daniel>Well, the authors rely heavily on a concept called epistemic debt.</v>

110
00:13:16.510 --> 00:13:17.231
<v Maya>Epistemic debt.</v>

111
00:13:17.451 --> 00:13:32.349
<v Daniel>Yeah. It was coined by Unseta and colleagues in a 2026 Nature Machine Intelligence paper. Generative AI fundamentally compresses the time it takes to produce something. A report that used to take three days to write now takes 10 seconds. But AI does not shrink the</v>

112
00:13:32.389 --> 00:13:34.291
<v Daniel>time it takes to understand that report.</v>

113
00:13:34.887 --> 00:13:49.355
<v Maya>Okay, let's ground this in a scenario you've probably lived through. Imagine you ask the AI to write a 10-page financial projection for a new project. It takes five seconds. But you have a meeting with the CEO in an hour, and you don't actually know the math inside that document.</v>

114
00:13:49.615 --> 00:13:50.935
<v Daniel>Because the AI is a black box.</v>

115
00:13:51.176 --> 00:14:05.835
<v Maya>Exactly. You are in epistemic debt. So what do you do? You panic message your work bestie, Dave in accounting, begging him to jump on a 15-minute call to tell you if the AI's numbers actually make sense. You just saved three days of typing, but you generated an</v>

116
00:14:05.876 --> 00:14:06.736
<v Maya>emergency meeting.</v>

117
00:14:06.836 --> 00:14:20.900
<v Daniel>That perfectly illustrates the concept. You have borrowed knowledge from the machine, incurring a debt. To pay that debt down, you have to verify the output. You pull in colleagues to help you interpret it, check the logic, and legitimize it before you present it upward.</v>

118
00:14:21.570 --> 00:14:29.138
<v Maya>keep this short, but this connects directly to that Boston Consulting Group field experiment by Delacroix, Mollick, and colleagues, right? The jagged technological frontier.</v>

119
00:14:29.318 --> 00:14:42.052
<v Daniel>Exactly. They found AI is amazing when a task is inside its capabilities, but disastrous when a task falls just outside its frontier. And because that frontier is invisible, you absolutely need human checking.</v>

120
00:14:42.805 --> 00:14:45.507
<v Maya>So the human network acts as the quality assurance layer.</v>

121
00:14:45.707 --> 00:15:00.676
<v Daniel>It does. But this brings us to the very heart of the paper and a fascinating paradox in the data. We saw networks get broader and less clustered, which fits the idea of reaching out for independent checks. But the researchers also found that adopters</v>

122
00:15:00.716 --> 00:15:05.139
<v Daniel>put more of their time into colleagues with whom they have long shared histories.</v>

123
00:15:05.619 --> 00:15:06.640
<v Maya>Interesting.</v>

124
00:15:07.676 --> 00:15:15.881
<v Daniel>The authors call this preferential reinforcement, and they link it to sociologist David Krakard's famous theory on the strength of strong ties.</v>

125
00:15:16.782 --> 00:15:31.691
<v Maya>Here is where it gets really interesting, because this is the part that should make any manager nervous. If I'm going to Dave in accounting with my massive AI-generated document, am I doing it to get a rigorous independent check? Or am I going to</v>

126
00:15:31.711 --> 00:15:37.195
<v Maya>Dave because we've worked together for five years, he trusts me, and I know he'll just rubber stamp it so I can get it off my desk.</v>

127
00:15:37.787 --> 00:15:52.518
<v Daniel>And that is the ultimate tension here. The data cannot tell us which one it is. Because we only have metadata, not the content of the conversations, leaning heavily on strong ties could be rigorous checking or it could absolutely be rubber</v>

128
00:15:52.538 --> 00:15:52.878
<v Daniel>stamping.</v>

129
00:15:53.859 --> 00:16:01.865
<v Maya>Wow. And if trust lowers scrutiny, then collaboration could actually spread the epistemic debt instead of paying it off.</v>

130
00:16:02.446 --> 00:16:02.926
<v Daniel>Exactly.</v>

131
00:16:03.802 --> 00:16:15.642
<v Maya>Like Dave signs off on the AI report, so it becomes official company policy, but Dave only signed off on it because he trusts you, and you only submitted it because the AI sounded confident. Nobody actually knows if the math is right.</v>

132
00:16:16.070 --> 00:16:31.054
<v Daniel>It is a massive organizational risk. The broader, less clustered network fits the independent check story, while leaning on strong ties introduces this vulnerability. So if organizations want to avoid spreading epistemic debt, they have to establish</v>

133
00:16:31.114 --> 00:16:35.915
<v Daniel>cultural norms that explicitly reward questioning AI-assisted work.</v>

134
00:16:36.455 --> 00:16:42.817
<v Maya>Right. They need norms around disclosing when AI was used, and they need to create clear accountability and review roles.</v>

135
00:16:43.485 --> 00:16:50.688
<v Daniel>The technology changes the output, but the organization has to adapt its social structures to manage the risk of that output.</v>

136
00:16:50.988 --> 00:16:58.151
<v Maya>Absolutely. But before we rewrite the rule book on modern work entirely based on this, what are the blind spots of this study?</v>

137
00:16:58.671 --> 00:17:06.575
<v Daniel>Well, the authors list several important caveats. First, this is only one business unit of one specific multinational company.</v>

138
00:17:06.815 --> 00:17:07.015
<v Maya>Right.</v>

139
00:17:07.675 --> 00:17:11.477
<v Daniel>Second, as we discussed, it only covers the first three months after adoption.</v>

140
00:17:12.233 --> 00:17:20.440
<v Maya>So it could just be a temporary adjustment period. I mean, when a new software drops, half the company just spends three weeks having meetings to figure out where the save button is.</v>

141
00:17:20.580 --> 00:17:35.270
<v Daniel>Exactly. And because there is no content data, that is a perfectly valid alternative explanation. Maybe they are just having conversations about the new tool itself. Or maybe time saved on drafting simply goes into meetings. Or coordination is needed for reasons totally</v>

142
00:17:35.350 --> 00:17:36.690
<v Daniel>unrelated to doubting the AI.</v>

143
00:17:37.010 --> 00:17:37.310
<v Maya>Right.</v>

144
00:17:37.830 --> 00:17:42.552
<v Daniel>Also, jobs with different structures might experience the exact opposite effect. Like software development.</v>

145
00:17:43.272 --> 00:17:55.295
<v Maya>Oh, yeah. Like the background research on GitHub Copilot. That showed developers basically putting their headphones on and coding more tasks. They might actually talk less because their work is highly individual and the code either compiles or it doesn't.</v>

146
00:17:56.055 --> 00:18:10.445
<v Daniel>The interdependency of the work matters immensely. But even with those limitations, the authors observed one macro-level change that is hard to ignore. The overall network of this business unit became more integrated. There were shorter paths between people.</v>

147
00:18:11.246 --> 00:18:13.908
<v Maya>The organization as a whole became more tightly knit.</v>

148
00:18:14.128 --> 00:18:14.288
<v Daniel>Yes.</v>

149
00:18:15.059 --> 00:18:29.490
<v Maya>which defies everything we feared about this technology. To summarize the core takeaway for you listening, Gen AI, contrary to popular belief, did not displace human connection in this study. It didn't turn us into isolated hermits. It fundamentally</v>

150
00:18:29.611 --> 00:18:35.295
<v Maya>reorganized our networks, driving people to seek validation, interpretation, and judgment from their peers.</v>

151
00:18:36.216 --> 00:18:43.982
<v Daniel>The burden of work shifted from generating outputs to getting those outputs accepted. And verification remains an inherently social process.</v>

152
00:18:44.348 --> 00:18:59.114
<v Maya>But it leaves us with some really grounded, open questions. Is this just a novelty effect that will wear off once we trust the AI more? Are these extra hours of meetings actually productive validation, or are we just drowning in coordination</v>

153
00:18:59.214 --> 00:19:04.536
<v Maya>overload? And what does this mean for managers teaching knowledge workers to use AI responsibly?</v>

154
00:19:05.257 --> 00:19:10.539
<v Daniel>Finding the answers to those questions will really dictate how we design organizations for the foreseeable future.</v>

155
00:19:11.337 --> 00:19:25.533
<v Maya>So as you head into your next meeting or prompt your AI assistant for a first draft, I want to leave you with a final thought to mull over. If AI compresses the time it takes to produce work but increases the time we spend collaborating to validate it,</v>

156
00:19:26.314 --> 00:19:31.320
<v Maya>does the true value of human knowledge work shift entirely from production to judgment?</v>

157
00:19:31.921 --> 00:19:33.623
<v Daniel>A profound dynamic to consider.</v>

158
00:19:34.083 --> 00:19:39.510
<v Maya>Thank you so much for joining us for this deep dive. Keep questioning, keep learning, and we'll catch you next time.</v>
